WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.950 --> 00:00:02.480
- [Narrator] When Pfizer first reported

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:05.130
the efficacy of their COVID-19 vaccine,

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.370
a staggering 95%,

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:10.240
many thought that would
mark an end to the pandemic,

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:11.800
but that hasn't happened.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:13.730
Vaccination rates have remained low

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:15.340
in certain parts of the world,

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:17.920
despite multiple available vaccines.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.180
And there's also been reports
of breakthrough cases,

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.480
where fully-vaccinated people get COVID.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:27.000
And now, recent unpublished
data from Israel showed,

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.160
that the Pfizer vaccine
was only 40% effective

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:32.970
at reducing the risk of symptomatic COVID.

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:36.400
So if the original trials
showed high efficacy,

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.380
why did a large portion of
people in this Israeli study

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:40.500
get COVID?

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.610
Here's why those two
numbers are so far apart

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:46.123
and why neither of them
can tell the full story.

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:49.290
To understand this gap,

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:51.220
first, you need to
understand the difference

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:53.363
between efficacy and effectiveness.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:57.350
Vaccine efficacy refers to
how well a vaccine performs

00:00:57.350 --> 00:00:59.830
under ideal, tightly
controlled conditions,

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:01.630
like clinical trials.

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:04.380
For example, during trials for COVID-19,

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:07.200
vaccines were given to
people on a tight schedule.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.960
It also has to do with what
the researchers were studying.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.500
Here, they were looking at
how well vaccines prevented

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:15.710
symptomatic infections in
a limited number of people,

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:18.760
not whether they prevented
infection altogether,

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.140
or kept people from being contagious.

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:23.810
And with COVID-19, it's
possible to be infected

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:25.250
and not show symptoms.

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:27.490
- What determines how efficacious,

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:30.490
or how well the vaccine works in a trial,

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:31.940
is really two things.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:34.000
- [Narrator] Lisa Lee has
studied the spread of viruses

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.070
for 30 years,

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:37.420
and has held leadership roles at the CDC.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:40.080
- It's how contagious the virus is,

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.850
how many people an infected
person might infect

00:01:45.676 --> 00:01:47.150
in the absence of that vaccine.

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:52.100
And it also depends a lot
on the host, that is people.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:55.830
How well can the vaccine stimulate

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:58.050
the right immune response for people?

00:01:58.050 --> 00:01:59.230
- [Narrator] In Pfizer study,

00:01:59.230 --> 00:02:01.730
the company tested about 44,000 people

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:03.670
for symptomatic COVID-19,

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:07.600
and only 170 develop the disease
with at least one symptom.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:10.170
And of those, just eight
had been vaccinated,

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:12.890
while 162 had gotten the placebo.

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:16.610
This is what added up to
that 95% efficacy rate.

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:19.870
This by the way, means that
in a vaccinated population,

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:23.120
95% fewer people will contract the disease

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.250
when they come in contact with the virus,

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:28.850
not that each individual is 95% immune.

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:33.040
But that efficacy rate was
calculated during a specific time

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:35.890
and under a specific set of circumstances.

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:37.610
Many people were staying at home,

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:39.800
avoiding large gatherings, wearing masks,

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.670
and the virus hadn't mutated as much.

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:44.970
Vaccine effectiveness, on the other hand,

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:47.530
is how well a vaccine
works in the real world,

00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:49.270
outside of clinical settings,

00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:51.040
where it's harder to control variables

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:52.560
that were constant in a study,

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.190
like when people get their vaccines.

00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:56.910
- You know, the real
world is kind of messy.

00:02:56.910 --> 00:03:00.350
Not everybody gets their
vaccines at the exact interval.

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:03.180
Not everybody has the same
kind of immune system.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:04.980
- [Narrator] And behavior is changing too.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:07.430
In many places, businesses are open,

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:09.380
and large gatherings are happening again.

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:11.970
This is part of why
efficacy numbers in trials

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:13.930
are often higher than the effectiveness

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:15.430
we see in the real world.

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:17.730
And what makes this all
the more complicated

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:20.350
is that the virus itself
is still evolving.

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:21.480
The Delta variant,

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:24.030
which has become dominant
in multiple countries,

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:27.130
is more transmissible than
earlier strains of the virus,

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:30.030
which means that more people
will come in contact with it.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:31.070
It became dominant,

00:03:31.070 --> 00:03:33.290
months after the vaccines
had been authorized,

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:35.450
and millions of doses
had been distributed.

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:37.400
That means our current shots were designed

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:39.950
for an earlier version of the coronavirus

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:42.590
and don't work as well against Delta.

00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:44.320
This is part of why we're hearing more

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:45.660
about breakthrough cases.

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:49.540
U.S. states counted over
190,000 breakthrough cases

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:52.730
between January 1st and
early August this year.

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:55.950
That's among millions who
are already vaccinated.

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:58.000
And remember that Israeli study,

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:00.820
data was collected for
it in June and July,

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:02.510
when Delta was dominant there.

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:04.340
Health officials saw breakthrough cases

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:07.080
were more likely among people
who had gotten the vaccine

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:09.710
early on, in January or February.

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:11.530
This is partly because antibodies,

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:13.770
which the body produces after the vaccine

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:15.730
to protect itself against the virus,

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:17.600
naturally decrease over time.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.140
That, along with the
unknowns about variants,

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:22.570
is a big reason why the
Biden administration

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:24.834
announced new vaccine recommendations.

00:04:24.834 --> 00:04:28.570
- We are announcing our plan
to stay ahead of this virus,

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:33.030
by being prepared to offer
COVID-19 booster shots,

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:36.710
to fully-vaccinated
adults, 18 years and older.

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:38.180
- What we want to do is make sure

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:40.890
that we keep that immunity high enough,

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:42.900
so when it starts to wane,

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:44.080
we want to make sure that people

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:46.170
are able to get that third shot.

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:47.730
- [Narrator] That should
increase the chances

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:49.900
that the vaccines prevent severe illness

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:53.150
that could then lead to
hospitalization and death.

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:56.090
Still, there is some debate
among public health experts

00:04:56.090 --> 00:04:58.640
as to whether booster
shots are needed right now,

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:01.720
that's because antibodies are
not the body's only defense

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:02.830
against the virus.

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:04.640
There are also certain immune cells

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:07.290
that prepare the body
to fight off pathogens,

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:09.010
and those may not be waning.

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:11.470
And while breakthrough
cases continued to occur,

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:14.410
experts say that these
cases are usually mild

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:16.510
and don't result in severe illness.

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:18.650
Let's go back to that Israeli data

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:20.170
that showed that the Pfizer vaccine

00:05:20.170 --> 00:05:23.840
is now only 40% effective
against symptomatic COVID.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:26.870
Well, that same study
showed that the vaccine,

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:31.190
had a 91% effectiveness against
hospitalization and death.

00:05:31.190 --> 00:05:32.930
- The vaccine really,

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:36.540
what it does is help us
slow the speeding car,

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:39.810
and it helps us to reduce transmission.

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:43.430
And that is what's gonna
get us out of this pandemic.

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:44.910
- [Narrator] In addition to vaccines,

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:48.430
experts also recommend preventive
measures like mask wearing

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:51.450
and social distancing to
keep the virus from spreading

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:52.884
and mutating further.

00:05:52.884 --> 00:05:55.384
(tense music)

